@page "/HowItWorks/takeovers-and-spin-offs"
@using InvestmentTaxCalculator.Components

<HowItWorksGuideLayout PageAddress="HowItWorks/takeovers-and-spin-offs">
    <ChildContent>
        <div class="section">
            <p>
                Brokers rarely report the details needed for these events, such as the market values used to split the
                cost, so you enter them yourself on the <a href="corporate-actions" class="link-styled">Corporate Actions and
                Events</a> page.
            </p>
        </div>

        <div class="section">
            <h2 class="section-title">Takeovers and mergers</h2>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    You enter the old company, the acquiring company, how many new shares you get for each old share, and any
                    cash paid.
                </li>
                <li>
                    For a share-for-share exchange, the old company's pool is emptied and its total cost moves to the new
                    shares. The new shares are treated as if bought when the old ones were, so there is no disposal
                    (TCGA 1992 s127 and s135).
                </li>
                <li>
                    If you also receive cash, you enter the market value of the new shares as well. If the cash is small
                    (£3,000 or less, or no more than 5% of the total value), you can choose to deduct it from the cost
                    carried to the new shares instead of treating it as a disposal (s122). For takeovers this option is off
                    unless you tick it. Otherwise the cash is a part disposal: the cost used is the old pool's cost ×
                    cash ÷ (cash + value of the new shares), and the rest of the cost moves to the new shares.
                </li>
                <li>
                    A takeover does not link the two companies for matching. For example, a sale of the old shares is not
                    matched with a purchase of the new shares in the following 30 days.
                </li>
            </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="section">
            <h2 class="section-title">Spin-offs (demergers)</h2>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    You enter the parent company, the new company, how many new shares you get for each parent share, and
                    the market value of the parent and new shares at the spin-off date.
                </li>
                <li>
                    The parent's pool cost is split by those market values. The new company gets cost × new value ÷
                    (parent value + new value), and the parent keeps the rest. The number of shares in the parent does not
                    change.
                </li>
                <li>
                    New shares are kept to four decimal places. If you received cash for a fraction of a share, they are
                    rounded down to whole shares and the cash is treated as for stock splits: if it is small it is
                    deducted from cost by default, otherwise it is a part disposal.
                </li>
            </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="card mb-4">
            <div class="card-body">
                <h2 class="h5 card-title">Worked example: spin-off</h2>
                <p>
                    You hold 100 parent shares costing £6,000 and receive 50 shares in the new company. On the spin-off date
                    the parent shares are worth £8,000 and the new shares £2,000.
                </p>
                <p class="mb-0">
                    The new company takes £6,000 × 2,000 ÷ 10,000 = <strong>£1,200</strong> of cost for its 50 shares, and the
                    parent keeps <strong>£4,800</strong> for its 100 shares.
                </p>
            </div>
        </div>
    </ChildContent>
    <FurtherReading>
        <li><a href="https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/1992/12/section/127" class="link-styled" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">TCGA 1992 s127</a>: reorganisations</li>
        <li><a href="https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/1992/12/section/135" class="link-styled" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">TCGA 1992 s135</a>: exchange of securities</li>
        <li><a href="https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/1992/12/section/122" class="link-styled" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">TCGA 1992 s122</a>: small capital distributions</li>
        <li><a href="HowItWorks/share-identities" class="link-styled">Share identities</a>: when the company changes its ticker or ISIN without a takeover</li>
    </FurtherReading>
</HowItWorksGuideLayout>
